      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bring the water to a simmer in a medium saucepan. Dissolve the miso paste (or doenjang) into the water by pressing it through a small sieve or whisking vigorously. Add the gochujang if using."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the garlic, onion, and courgette to the broth. Simmer over medium heat for 8 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the mushrooms and tofu. Simmer gently for a further 8 minutes. Do not boil vigorously or the tofu will break apart."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Taste and adjust the seasoning. Add more miso if the broth needs more depth."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the spring onion and drizzle the sesame oil over the top. Ladle into bowls and serve immediately with steamed rice. Garnish with sliced red chilli if liked."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Stew is too salty",
              "fix": "Doenjang and miso are both very salty. Add water 100 ml at a time until the balance is right. Never add more paste without tasting first."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Tofu is falling apart",
              "fix": "Simmer very gently rather than boiling. Add the tofu towards the end and handle it as little as possible."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Stew lacks depth",
              "fix": "Increase the miso by another tablespoon. A splash of fish sauce also deepens the flavour if you are not keeping the dish vegetarian."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Doenjang jjigae is made in Korean homes every day. It is on the table at breakfast as often as at dinner. Many Koreans consider it the most comforting food they know. The recipe changes slightly with the season. Courgette and tofu appear in summer versions. Potato and dried radish are more common in winter ones."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
